{"title":"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Remark\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remark</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/4/d745b81add053aa31f45d72cb9a898e3441e74bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, a_2, a_3, \\dots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"95\" height=\"11\" /> be an infinite sequence of positive integers greater than 1.\n</p><p>Suppose that for all positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, the number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36cfb07e4911e7b4f994b858f3d47604ddbee438.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> is the smallest positive integer greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/8/b08555e71e4aa0335936fecd3cdfb45a0040f1a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(a_{n+1}, a_i) &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"135\" height=\"20\" /> for every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/8/4c8fda85b509471f117cb6e73b2680da394de5fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i = 1, 2, \\dots, n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"107\" height=\"16\" />. \n</p><p>Prove that there exist positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> such that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/c/03cc91d3d9debb09b31c0f2ddee3a807762c5bf8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_{n+T}  = a_n + L\\]\" width=\"115\" height=\"17\" />\nfor every positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. \n</p><p>(Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/5/a75c1d5e24ac094e4b383e8c7034d2bddb95c570.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"71\" height=\"20\" /> denotes the greatest common divisor of positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />.)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/VyaOJAzIwbQ\">https://youtu.be/VyaOJAzIwbQ</a> (by Dedekind Cuts)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><b>Lemma 1:</b> For all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> the set of prime divisors of each of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/8/e78d29e8ba89d05ec741fbe20ba062f516773357.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, \\dots, a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"11\" /> is a fixed finite set.\n</p><p><b>Proof of Lemma 1:</b>\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/c/54c51a4acedda645113145d008dbfb50d35f1da3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> be the set of all prime numbers that divide at least one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/8/e78d29e8ba89d05ec741fbe20ba062f516773357.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, \\dots, a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"11\" />. If such an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36cfb07e4911e7b4f994b858f3d47604ddbee438.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> exists, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36cfb07e4911e7b4f994b858f3d47604ddbee438.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> must share at least one common prime divisor with every previous term. That implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/3/253476f22ad529da3eb644c252852812d755b193.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{a prime in} \\ a_{n+1} \\  \\in P_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"166\" height=\"18\" />. Notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36cfb07e4911e7b4f994b858f3d47604ddbee438.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> however is minimalistic, hence if such a sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> were to exist, by the Pigeonhole Principle, eventually new primes will no longer be introduced hence we \"cycle\" primes within each next term of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. Hence there exists a set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/7/6379abae58190cd436ad18ee80a8a8d7644b9683.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{P} = \\{p_1, p_2, \\dots, p_k\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"157\" height=\"20\" /> of finite prime divisors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p><b>Lemma 2:</b> If an integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/f/def9f44e7031a97b9ae65f601ac83d87f1fc18ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\in a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" /> works, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/7/fa7328dfaf826046e02a9ce7195acfc1f2915710.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x + M \\in a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"86\" height=\"16\" /> also works, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/f/30fcf9d23eb09074172b0c95ee19b9396902cf32.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M = \\prod \\mathcal{P}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"86\" height=\"31\" />.\n</p><p><b>Proof of Lemma 2:</b>\n</p><p>Denote <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/b/8fb4816ceb8afeb9cc1429f28dde8a921c7bee2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M = p_1p_2\\dots p_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"124\" height=\"18\" />. If the term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/f/def9f44e7031a97b9ae65f601ac83d87f1fc18ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\in a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" /> works, it has some prime factorization from which one value in the general prime pool <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> works. Then, we may simply factor this (or these) primes out, yielding a new number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/a/25afc5fc6128dafe17d5f37bec78b1c38b9bba5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"54\" height=\"16\" /> who also satisfies the properties for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. \n</p><p><b>2026 IMO/Q6:</b>\n</p><p>Suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" /> is in this set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. Consider <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/9/d79eefc12758e88e133a42df071291c6f9b0b2f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L = M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"58\" height=\"14\" />, and notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/f/75f8a7619eb253c00a1217eb8c3ae01e09c9a3b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n + L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"17\" /> is also in this set. Obviously, as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/6/3f60deb802f9891c234443bf024f4528c9879e9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/3/523d19318931a9e9ac402ce625d00f87b8100d92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n + L &gt; a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"17\" />, it follows that then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/1/ac18d0ab4591587d8721d10fc4b69711835b6dec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n+L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"17\" /> occurs later in the sequence than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" />. If this number appears exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> later in the sequence, then it follows all elements of sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> satisfy\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/c/03cc91d3d9debb09b31c0f2ddee3a807762c5bf8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_{n+T} = a_n + L\\]\" width=\"115\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>for some integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remark\">Remark</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Remark\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Quite a hard problem, living up to the ideal Q6 standards. Great problem to end the 2026 IMO.&#160;:&gt;\n</p><p>As for the problem, notice that the next term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36cfb07e4911e7b4f994b858f3d47604ddbee438.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> is determined solely based on the prime divisors of each of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/8/e78d29e8ba89d05ec741fbe20ba062f516773357.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, \\dots, a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"11\" />. This proof is left as an exercise to the reader.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/7/c87e77b076fcac95df64e7508ec52f280887174b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\mathcal{A} = \\{a_1, a_2, \\dots\\}, \\quad P(m) = \\{\\text{all prime factors of } m\\}.\\]\" width=\"405\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>By the recurrence condition, any two terms in the sequence are not coprime, i.e.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/c/62c91525cab65a23d9aef0e799231f9e285cb09d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[i \\neq j \\implies P(a_i) \\cap P(a_j) \\neq \\emptyset. \\tag{1}\\]\" width=\"386\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>If an integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/1/4a10b2d7d3c71faa7efff494f6f9efd091bd2101.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\ge a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"16\" /> does not belong to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/7/0b7bf42db84e4d2dbb7bf55476c12bf9f6233bc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> such that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/f/8cf75af427a32a207a2ea4ba20a1cb41c198f6e1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_k &lt; m &lt; a_{k+1}.\\]\" width=\"120\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>By the minimality of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/b/51bb8f97be092c275f4636c0782c3b93b3c69df8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{k+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"32\" height=\"12\" />, there must be some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/e/92eca6c4646a7191efe57e94c29bc29d3dd6c976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i \\le k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"17\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/7/887e4807f23c758e0d4ff4593249bb3d217400c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(m, a_i) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"116\" height=\"20\" />. Hence\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/8/be80efdd3df80e19c60bd7faba9bd2c3fc4eaf94.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[m \\notin \\mathcal{A} \\implies \\exists a_i &lt; m, \\quad P(m) \\cap P(a_i) = \\emptyset. \\tag{2}\\]\" width=\"437\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>From this we obtain an important property: if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/e/f2e330b7f3e48c0948f6c9b707c370e3cbc30ebe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\in \\mathcal{A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/1/4a10b2d7d3c71faa7efff494f6f9efd091bd2101.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\ge a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"16\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/e/95e77254ac229ac11f0e97eb58ecb2bbaa631b1d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x) \\subseteq P(m)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"109\" height=\"20\" />, then\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/d/68d52874988122d162cafb5fb95316e31d548cee.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[m \\in \\mathcal{A}. \\tag{3}\\]\" width=\"297\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>Otherwise, by (2) there exists <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/2/412787c048e28774dc63fc27db42dc52ca858de7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> coprime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />, hence coprime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, contradicting (1).\n</p><p>Now examine <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/a/75a2d6d47afcf79e8493b06e33a20204d7102354.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, a_2, \\dots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"70\" height=\"11\" /> in order and select \"key terms\": first select <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/b/1ab39d761413804680d26d972381f028001562f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" />; if previously no key term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> satisfies\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/d/58de0e8ef7aa4b3090df7181d52cb25dc3e97e2b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P(b) \\subseteq P(a_n),\\]\" width=\"111\" height=\"18\" />\nthen select <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" />. Clearly, every term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" /> contains all prime factors of some key term.\n</p><p>We now prove that there are only finitely many key terms. Let\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/a/36a047140c84ffbf6471b6171299d22430c3988a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[Q = P(a_1), \\quad q_0 = \\max Q, \\quad C = a_1 q_0.\\]\" width=\"298\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Suppose a key term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/6/ae6182332f130a21d07b195901b0badfed21221c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &gt; C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"15\" /> contains a prime factor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> that has never appeared in any previous key term. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/b/1ab39d761413804680d26d972381f028001562f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" /> is a previous key term, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/f/d8f9b05e32e7213a40e37ea4856923ea5642907a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\notin Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"48\" height=\"19\" />. Also, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/c/fccdf6230e6635d044ae0111fb3f71ae9325db1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(x, a_1) &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"20\" />, we can take\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/8/1f8fa1c5440e549bf2385c51dedfd80625f66732.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[q \\in P(x) \\cap Q, \\quad q \\neq p.\\]\" width=\"174\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Let\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/4/75413c7c4b0bc47094b8d5a1132d8fd5ef0c8cce.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[S = P(x) \\setminus \\{p\\}, \\quad r = \\prod_{i \\in S} i.\\]\" width=\"212\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>For any previous key term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />, by (1) we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/d/cade4d1778f4e578c92e025198d25b940417586b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(b) \\cap P(x) \\neq \\emptyset$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"130\" height=\"20\" />; since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/8/d080840de544b1bf850c7485a63076b47376454b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\notin P(b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"69\" height=\"20\" />, we get\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/e/06e81c4954de94070bc6f18ebc739ded94b3d74d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P(b) \\cap S \\neq \\emptyset. \\tag{4}\\]\" width=\"321\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>Every previous term contains all prime factors of some previous key term, so any integer whose set of prime factors is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is not coprime to any term before <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/8/d88a989f44b70280c030362bceadb26132f04656.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r \\ge a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"16\" />, take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/e/98e09d133ef3ca7e7034e67f94291f1a87c230ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/c/53cfcd58738ed0359931688fa86d1ef91d4ac57e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$rp \\mid x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"45\" height=\"20\" />, we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/e/a7e74cac72e8ae615c335648bda354b7cd5309ae.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_1 \\le y = r &lt; x.\\]\" width=\"121\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/90651acd30c9ef3673c9726f6b3067ae2b29c0f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r &lt; a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"15\" />, take the smallest positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/d/e0d2bf360290fd61d1c1557e763f2622363b3d35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"13\" /> such that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/8/228abeb9a883e8cec243a41de71366f646ddcdbd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[y = rq^t \\ge a_1.\\]\" width=\"101\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/c/5dc6cdf6d27284dba6b31340f7db29a6b1d70963.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$rq^{t-1} &lt; a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"83\" height=\"20\" />, we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/5/1f510353fb43d85587da403a50e5a27fe7f86fc2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_1 \\le y = rq^t &lt; qa_1 \\le qa_1 = C &lt; x.\\]\" width=\"275\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>So in both cases we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/1/9b179dcc53677f88fefb9d4e3573498b0ae65f0e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_1 \\le y &lt; x, \\quad P(y) = S. \\tag{5}\\]\" width=\"362\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>By (4), <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is not coprime to any term before <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />; by (2), we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/1/ef1c8bcc1a85d0e16623985cdf51e4b6d12744f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y \\in \\mathcal{A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"48\" height=\"18\" />. Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> contains all prime factors of some previous key term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />, so\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/9/22987108fb0e8048de9874e4df4e4a97f4512402.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P(b) \\subseteq P(y) = S \\subseteq P(x),\\]\" width=\"201\" height=\"18\" />\ncontradicting that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is a key term.\n</p><p>Therefore, no key term greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> can introduce a new prime factor. There are only finitely many key terms not exceeding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />; let their combined set of prime factors be the finite set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/b/dfb064112b6c94470339f6571f69d07afc1c024c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" />. By the conclusion above, all subsequent key terms <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> satisfy\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/4/dd4aa8cacc3345d389e7afd3a103e6320cdef1dd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P(x) \\subseteq K.\\]\" width=\"82\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/b/dfb064112b6c94470339f6571f69d07afc1c024c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" /> has only finitely many subsets, and different key terms must have different sets of prime factors, the total number of key terms is finite. Let all key terms be\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/1/a91c57004d5f3fb5ef52355e93bb9e9403bdbb08.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[b_1, b_2, \\dots, b_s,\\]\" width=\"97\" height=\"16\" />\nand define\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/4/df4f32838cd0ff851eda50bcd4baca3f6f66d70e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[d_j = \\prod_{p \\in P(b_j)} p = \\operatorname{rad}(b_j) \\quad (1 \\le j \\le s). \\tag{6}\\]\" width=\"413\" height=\"42\" />\n</p><p>By the definition of key terms and property (3), for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/1/4a10b2d7d3c71faa7efff494f6f9efd091bd2101.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\ge a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"16\" />,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/4/7d4a55c96f55e64c69415c49bf93bd2e45d1e5e5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[m \\in \\mathcal{A} \\iff \\exists j \\in \\{1, \\dots, s\\}, \\quad d_j \\mid m. \\tag{7}\\]\" width=\"418\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>Let\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/9/7f921e5a60423d8720226378c06ae99747f050ad.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[L = \\operatorname{lcm}(d_1, d_2, \\dots, d_s). \\tag{8}\\]\" width=\"359\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Since each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/a/3aab0ab6a2bb7235f10be940b02d56b86efd4c23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d_j \\mid L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"46\" height=\"20\" />, by (7) we get\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/5/375063ac51ead6144ac392e59b7c9987d614fb38.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[m \\in \\mathcal{A} \\iff m + L \\in \\mathcal{A} \\quad (m \\ge a_1). \\tag{9}\\]\" width=\"411\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Finally, let\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/b/d6bb6cc708b25bcee1a2f1a6b6970bb2e172f158.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[T = \\#(\\mathcal{A} \\cap [a_1, a_1 + L)). \\tag{10}\\]\" width=\"366\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>By (9), the map <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/a/1ca7aeab1d8adc6b20bba685aeadff5608c27d76.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\mapsto m + L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"97\" height=\"16\" /> gives an order-preserving bijection from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/7/0b7bf42db84e4d2dbb7bf55476c12bf9f6233bc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/b/92bfe3692ca9927f2caf7ff9c1a3d3dba283834f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{A} \\cap [a_1 + L, \\infty)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"127\" height=\"20\" />. Therefore, after removing the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> terms, the remaining terms are\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/8/7b88040a0c295a037747c03ab6e7560cececb563.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_1 + L, \\quad a_2 + L, \\quad a_3 + L, \\dots\\]\" width=\"231\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>Thus, for every positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/6/a063d296227c5dcda5103712d2f9dac3fd04320e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_{n+T} = a_n + L.\\]\" width=\"119\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>Hence proved.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/a/f7a7be3e92b4b20997a32af195fc43a660b279bd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\boxed{\\text{There exist positive integers } T \\text{ and } L \\text{ such that } a_{n+T} = a_n + L.}\\]\" width=\"485\" height=\"28\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO\" title=\"2026 IMO\">2026 IMO</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions,_with_authors\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions, with authors\">IMO Problems and Solutions, with authors</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mathematics_competitions_resources\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Mathematics competitions resources\">Mathematics competitions resources</a></li></ul>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO\" title=\"2026 IMO\">2026 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b>Last Question</b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018023757\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.047 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.060 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 671/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 866/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 51/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 22878/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.856      1 Template:IMO_box\n100.00%    6.856      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:37211:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018023757 and revision id 287617. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}